# Do not edit these without asking

For any file below: **propose a diff and explain the reasoning. Do not apply
the change.** Every one of these has already caused a production incident.

| File | What it does | What went wrong before |
|---|---|---|
| `hooks/useCosmosSync.ts` | Settings + watchlist sync/restore | Wiped every alert subscription on re-login. Orphaned watchlists because IDs regenerate on restore |
| `store/useStore.ts` → `addAlert` / `addAlerts` | Cross-source dedup | Collapsed genuinely distinct alerts into one |
| `lib/alertDedup.ts` | Message normalisation + match keys | The `[+]` filtered-PR copy showed twice because the prefix wasn't stripped |
| `hooks/useSignalR.ts`, `hooks/useNewsHub.ts` | Live alert ingestion | Where most alert-loss bugs originate |
| `lib/marketCalendar.ts` + per-source `since` windows | "Since previous close" | Hid an entire session of news — a 13:45 PR and morning filings vanished |
| Anything touching `userKey`, auth, or identity | Login + per-user data | Settings and subscriptions wiped on re-login |

## Why these specifically

**They fail silently.** A broken dedup condition or a wrong date window does
not throw, does not log, and does not look different on screen. The alert
simply never appears, and nobody finds out until a trade is missed.

Normal review — "does the page still load?" — cannot catch this class of bug.
That is why these are gated on a human who knows the history, not on whether
the change looks reasonable.

## The specific trap

Do not "simplify", "tidy", or "optimise" a filter, a dedup check, or a date
comparison. The convoluted-looking conditions in these files are convoluted
because each clause fixes a real reported bug. Removing one looks like a
cleanup and reads as an improvement in review.
